#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""dayone.py - the Qwen4 day-one kit's one entry point (Karl 11-05: "One script, given a GGUF repo: pick the largest quant
whose experts fit about 72 GiB (prefer ISTA or Unsloth per the v3.1 result, leave that as a setting); check disk space and
download; pack the file and write a config with explicit helper slot counts; generate the exactness smoke test,
KL-vs-reference and speed-screen drivers. Dry-run every step that needs no GPU on Flash-Next as the stand-in.").
Written 11-05 before any Qwen4 release. No GPU, no engine, nothing installed; plan first, then each step on its own.

  dayone.py plan --repo OWNER/NAME[@REV] [--repo ...] [--local SHARD1 ...] --name NAME --out DIR [options]
      1. the repo's files at a PINNED commit (HF API, paginated; --listing REPO=FILE reads a saved listing instead),
         grouped by quant (split shards joined; MTP / engram / mmproj / imatrix files listed apart);
      2. headers: reused from --headers DIR or the files on disk; else (live) fetched by range requests
         (bringup/fetch_model.py fetch_head: grown until gguf_head parses, 206 + Content-Range required) - only for the
         quants a size pre-screen puts near the fit boundary (exact experts of the read quants, estimates for the rest);
      3. work/qwen4-kit/day1.py check on every quant with a header (types against the engine tree, the fit by Karl's rule,
         ties, the same-model swap test, the day-one config / params / commands);
      4. the pick: Karl's rule (the largest whose experts fit RAM + VRAM, ~72 GiB); a tie (equal expert bytes) is broken
         by --prefer ista|unsloth, else "quality decides" (--prefer any); --prefer auto (default) reads the v3.1 result
         (results/r23/ud31/decision.json: U BETTER THAN P -> unsloth, WORSE THAN P -> ista, else any). The documented
         default today: v3.1's UD-IQ3_XXS ties our ISTA IQ3_S on expert bytes (results/qwen4-kit/unsloth-v31/REPORT.md),
         and its quality gap (work/r23/gap-ud31.sh) has not run -> any -> quality decides;
      5. disk: free space after the download + the pack + the splice (if any) + the drafter conversion >= 100 GB;
      6. download plan: work/qwen4-kit/gentle_dl.py (pinned commit, checksummed, page-cache dropping, the 100 GB floor;
         Karl 11-05's default --window measure: at once, paused only while a gap is open, no fixed cap - a share of the
         line left to a live LDR run) - planned, never started by `plan`;
      7. pack plan: the release tree's tools/iq_pack.py (--compat-bf16 when day1 says so) under
         work/r23/nogap-run.sh (nice 19, freezes while a gap is open); STRATA_HC_Q8=1 in the config for a file that
         stores Q8_0 hyper-connections (Karl 11-05);
      8. configs: day1's config (P028x.json as the template) with EXPLICIT --expert-cache / --expert-cache-device1
         counts from the slot model (results/catchup/F1-frame-pools.md: --expert-cache N = a byte budget of N largest
         blobs, filled in profile order at each layer's own size; the helper is a count); the KL reference's config;
      9. the gap drivers (exactness smoke, KL vs the named reference, speed screen) from the templates below, in the
         series' conventions (pipefail, lock fds closed, preflight, port_gap4 begin / end, kcheck after every step, exit
         0 / 1 / 3 / 4), with work/qwen4-kit/q4_rules.py as their rules. NO .ready FILE IS WRITTEN: Claude reviews a
         driver and writes it before any gap may use it.
  dayone.py execute download|pack --plan DIR/plan.json [--go]   one step of a plan (without --go: print the command)
  dayone.py dryrun [--out results/qwen4-kit/dayone-dryrun]      every non-GPU step on Flash-Next as the stand-in,
      offline (the saved v3.1 listing + headers, our local files; the header fetch path exercised on a local file)
  dayone.py selftest                                            the slot model against F1's logged numbers, the pick

Read-only outside --out (and work/qwen4-kit/scratch-d/ in dryrun / selftest). Python: .venv/bin/python -I -B.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

